ng  reality of our electorate  and not just our election system i've  proposed in seneca 121  recognizing the fact that more than a  third or more than 30 percent  of the voters in the state of nevada  have decided not to be a member of the  republican or democratic parties  and the primaries most often decide  elections  i think we know that we've seen that um  and for the fact that  um our closed primary system doesn't  allow nonpartisans and minor party  members to participate  in the most important election of the  cycle they effectively get shut out of  represent  of choosing who represents them in their  government so i'm proposing an  open primary system uh it's a blanket  primary that allows  all voters to participate in primary  elections and it really changes how we  have to think about  our primaries we're no longer having a  primary that chooses a democratic  candidate and chooses a republican  candidate  we have all voters choose  to to choose who they want to go through  to the general election all candidates  go on one ballot  all voters get to vote the top two go  through to the general election and the  winner takes all  i think that's a system that recognizes  the fact that people are turning away  from the two major political parties  and it would give a stronger voice  to more people in this state to elect  their representatives in government  and hopefully bring us more together  well we have a lot more of that  interview with senator kikefer including  more on election reform suggestions and  how to try and fix  dieter you can see that interview at  eightnewsnow.com  so that open primary bill it's quite a  contrast to the straight party ticket  voting  uh that we talked about earlier in the  show that measure that's been proposed  we counted in fact at least 30 election  related bills so far  up in carson city others include putting  in ranked  choice voting moving to all mail and  expanding voting districts  another bill would amend the nevada  constitution to put some teeth  into the state's none of the above  option which is unique to nevada  if none of the above win a primary for  example then that party would have to  pick a candidate  not on the ballot and then if the  candidate won in the general election  a special election would be called we'll  keep an eye on all of that because  there's a lot of moving pieces  next on politics now working across the  aisle nevada democratic senator jackie  rosen  discusses her efforts to pair with a  republican senator  and try and get things done this is 8  news now  following another mass shooting  lawmakers debate whether more federal  gun laws are needed you're watching  politics now after two mass  shootings in the span of less than a  week gun control legislation is once  again coming up in congress  on monday 10 people were killed in  boulder colorado at a grocery store  last week eight people died in a string  of shootings in atlanta georgia  two bills have now passed the house in  washington d.c but now  they're in the bitterly divided senate  i've already committed to bringing  universal background checks legislation  to the floor of the senate when you  disarm  law-abiding citizens you make them more  likely to be victims  these shootings have brought the issues  of gun control to the forefront once  again in washington even before the  shootings happened in fact  democrats were considering federal gun  law changes and as you see our  washington correspondent alexander limon  is here to discuss  details of these proposals alexandra  what's included on capitol hill  the u.s house actually already passed  two different gun control measures one  calls for  near universal background checks with a  few exceptions that allows for transfers  between family members  what kind of opposition will democrats  see on capitol hill from republicans  what do lawmakers on the gop side have  to say about this  near total opposition from the  republican side and that's obviously  going to become  possibly probably an insurmountable  obstacle in the u.s senate because  obviously they need 60 votes to get any  of these measures through the senate and  democrats have nowhere near  that amount of votes or support for  these measures but despite that  democratic senate majority leader chuck  schumer has promised that he will be  calling for votes  on the senate floor on the background  check bills at a minimum  and he says that's because polls show  that 90 percent of americans  support these bills including a majority  of  republicans and a majority of gun owners  so  i think what's happening here is that  democrats are trying to exert some both  political and  public pressure on republicans to get  them officially on the record  on where they fall on these background  check bills and possibly even force them  into a situation where they're  involved in a talking filibuster on the  senate floor  over this issue alexander limon from  washington thank you very much  so congress is working on better  coordination now between state and  federal governments to try and stop  domestic terrorism  this week nevada attorney general a ron  ford testified before the house  subcommittee on intelligence and  counterterrorism  ford opened his statement reflecting on  our one october mass  shooting and the standoff at the bundy  ranch up in bunkerville  ford says we may need to change federal  laws to better prosecute domestic  tourists  there are no easy fixes in the fight  against domestic terrorism  with the rise of domestic violent  extremism there is no question that  something needs to change  so that we can ensure public safety  meant to say domestic terrorists of  course ford also says  we need to take a measured approach to  protect privacy free speech  and the right to bear arms two senators  say they're working together to buck the  polarizing trend in washington d.c  nevada democrat senator jackie rosen and  west virginia republican shelly moore  capito  on cbs this morning the pair said that  they were trying to find ways to work  together  we don't have to be disagreeable when we  disagree we can focus on the things we  agree on  that's why people send us here that's  what they expect from their leadership  you all make it sound so easy in terms  of  bipartisanship what are you doing that  some of your colleagues aren't i think  honestly if you're asking where i think  some of our other colleagues  unnamed could improve i think the way we  disagree  sometimes is very disrespectful so far  the pair have co-sponsored legislation  to improve  broadband internet access and expand  opportunities in science and technology  for young women  and what to watch looking forward here  on politics now we're waiting for the  next major deadline in the state  legislative  session up in carson city april 9th is  the date where bills have to make it out  of their first committee it's the first  key deadline and that's where a lot of  bills end up getting  killed at this point the legislature is  sticking to that deadline of april 9th  and has not moved it back we'll keep an  eye on that as things move forward  or don't move forward as it turns out in  carson city thank you very much for  watching us here on politics now this  week don't forget you can stay up to  date online with all the political news  eightnewsnow.com we'll see you right  back here on saturday thanks for  [Music]  watching  [Music]
 

